  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "State Of Gujarat vs Jitendrakumar Shantilal ... on 28 March, 2017 Author: A.G.Uraizee Bench: A.G.Uraizee R/Cr.A/1760/2009 Judgment In The High Court Of Gujarat At Ahmedabad Criminal Appeal No. 1760 of 2009 For Approval And Signature: Honourable Mr.Justice A.G.Uraizee ================================================================ 1",
    "facts": "2. The brief facts of the case of the prosecution, as set out before the learned Trial Court, read as under: 3. The short facts giving rise to present appeal is that complainantFood Inspector, Shri R.V. Dodiya, BhujKutchh visited the firm of accused on 30/06/1992 at about 13:00 hours in the presence of Panch and carried out the inspection. The complainant orally informed the accused that he wanted to analyze Besan before the Public Analyst and therefore to purchase the same and issue Notice in the prescribed Form No.6 as per Rule 12. Thereafter, the complainant had purchased 450 Grams of Besan and paid the amount for that. The complainant has obtained the signature of the accused in a receipt regarding receiving of amount. The complainant also obtained signatures of panchas in the panchnama he drew at the time of taking sample. Thereafter, he sent the samples to the Public Analyst, Rajkot for analysis. The Public Analyst after analyzing the sample opined as under: \"The sample of Besan is adulterated as it does not comply with the standards and provisions of PFA Act, 1954.\"",
    "arguments": "5. Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the aforesaid judgment and order passed by the learned Sessions Court, the appellantState has preferred the present Criminal Appeal. 7. Ms. Chandarana, the learned APP submits that the learned Magistrate acquitted the respondent on the ground of delay of one year and one month in lodging the complaint. The findings of the learned Magistrate that there are multiple violation for robbery provisions is not correct in view of the detailed oral evidence of the complainant. She, therefore, urges that the appeal may be allowed and the respondent may be convicted.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the aforesaid judgment and order passed by the learned Sessions Court, the appellantState has preferred the present Criminal Appeal. 7. Ms. Chandarana, the learned APP submits that the learned Magistrate acquitted the respondent on the ground of delay of one year and one month in lodging the complaint. The findings of the learned Magistrate that there are multiple violation for robbery provisions is not correct in view of the detailed oral evidence of the complainant. She, therefore, urges that the appeal may be allowed and the respondent may be convicted.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
